.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1206 AM PDT Sun Aug 9 2026
(Today and tonight)

The 00Z balloon observed a 925 mb temperature of 30.35 degrees
Celsius (third hottest for the date and time), 850 mb temperature of
28.75 degrees Celsius (second hottest for the date and time), and
500 mb height of 5970 meters (record for the date and time).
Satellite shows stratus clearing off the coast due to a
strengthening ACV-SFO surface pressure gradient. Northerly/offshore
flow down the Northern Sacramento Valley Sunday morning will help
push the marine layer back to the coast. Both of these factors
should support Sunday being the hottest day of this event; however,
if the marine layer prevails and northerly/offshore winds don`t
develop we could have a repeat of Saturday with our forecast being
too hot for the lower elevations. Nonetheless, temperatures will be
10 to 15 degrees above normal outside of the marine layer influence
with widespread moderate HeatRisk and areas of major HeatRisk in
the higher terrain. Minor coastal flooding is expected during the
highest high tide (evening/overnight) for bayshore locations
along the San Francisco Bay, San Pablo Bay, and Monterey Bay
through Thursday morning. Have opted to leave the Pacific Coast
out as it would be marginal and overnight if it occurred; however,
high tide may cutoff access to some beaches.

