.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 1206 AM PDT Sun Aug 9 2026

Near-critical fire weather conditions persist today across the
interior due to very hot and dry conditions. Strong thermal belting,
extended burn periods, elevated PIG, and near or record BI/ERC
values are expected. Temperatures will be 10 to 15 degrees above
normal with a compressed marine layer. Poor minimum daytime relative
humidity as low as 10% in the afternoons with little-to-no recovery
overnight is expected with nocturnal drying in the highest terrain.
Even with critically dry conditions, wind is the limiting factor
preventing this from being a Fire Weather Watch/Red Flag Warning
event. Outside of the diurnal sea breeze that will bring breezy
conditions in the afternoons and evenings through gaps and passes,
onshore flow will be light. Additionally, critical conditions are
not expected to meet coverage or duration requirements to warrant a
product. Localized elevated fire weather conditions in the highest
terrain can be expected Monday due to the combination of recent
hot/dry conditions and the increase in onshore flow. Depending how
the pattern and marine layer evolves, a headline may be warranted
and this could last through Wednesday.

